{"prompt": {"messages": [{"role": "system", "content": "You are a helpful assistant that repairs Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the given programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console."}, {"role": "user", "content": "Repair the following Python 2 code so that it correctly solves the programming problem. The code reads and writes I/O from the console. Your repaired code should implement the problem correctly, meaning that it should produce the correct output for any valid input.\nPrint your repaired code and also the confidence score you give to your repair. The confidence score should be a number between 0 and 1. The format of the output should be:\n<repaired_code>\n<your_repaired_code>\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n<your_confidence_score>\n</confidence>\nDo not provide any other text or markdown fences.\n\nHere is the problem description:\n<problem_description>\nSometimes one has to spell email addresses over the phone. Then one usually pronounces a dot as dot, an at sign as at. As a result, we get something like vasyaatgmaildotcom. Your task is to transform it into a proper email address (vasya@gmail.com). It is known that a proper email address contains only such symbols as . @ and lower-case Latin letters, doesn't start with and doesn't end with a dot. Also, a proper email address doesn't start with and doesn't end with an at sign. Moreover, an email address contains exactly one such symbol as @, yet may contain any number (possible, zero) of dots. You have to carry out a series of replacements so that the length of the result was as short as possible and it was a proper email address. If the lengths are equal, you should print the lexicographically minimal result. Overall, two variants of replacement are possible: dot can be replaced by a dot, at can be replaced by an at. \n</problem_description>\n\nHere is the Python 2 code you have to repair:\n<original_code>\n<your_rewritten_code_4>\na = raw_input()\n\n# Use a function to replace substrings within bounds\ndef replace_in_bounds(s, target, replacement, start=1):\n    result = list(s)\n    i = start\n    while i < len(result) - len(target) + 1:\n        if ''.join(result[i:i+len(target)]) == target:\n            result[i] = replacement[0]\n            for j in range(1, len(target)):\n                result[i+j] = ''\n            i += 1\n        else:\n            i += 1\n    return ''.join(result)\n\n# Replace 'dot' with '.'\na = replace_in_bounds(a, 'dot', '.', 1)\n\n# Replace 'at' with '@'\na = replace_in_bounds(a, 'at', '@', 1)\n\nprint a\n</your_rewritten_code_4>\n</original_code>\n"}], "temp": 0, "sample_size": 1, "logprobs": true, "model": "qwen/qwen3-coder-next"}, "response": {"samples": [{"content": "<repaired_code>\na = raw_input()\n\n# We need to find all occurrences of \"dot\" and \"at\" and replace them optimally\n# The key insight: we want the shortest possible result, which means replacing\n# as many substrings as possible. But we must ensure the result is a valid email.\n\n# Strategy: try all possible replacements for \"dot\" and \"at\" and find the valid\n# email with minimum length (and lexicographically smallest if tied)\n\nfrom itertools import product\n\ndef is_valid_email(s):\n    # Must contain exactly one '@'\n    if s.count('@') != 1:\n        return False\n    at_pos = s.index('@')\n    # Must not start or end with '@' or '.'\n    if s[0] == '@' or s[-1] == '@' or s[0] == '.' or s[-1] == '.':\n        return False\n    # Must contain only lowercase letters, '.', and '@'\n    valid_chars = set('abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz.@')\n    for c in s:\n        if c not in valid_chars:\n            return False\n    return True\n\ndef generate_replacements(s):\n    # Find all positions of \"dot\" and \"at\"\n    dot_positions = []\n    at_positions = []\n    \n    i = 0\n    while i < len(s) - 2:\n        if s[i:i+3] == 'dot':\n            dot_positions.append(i)\n            i += 3\n        elif s[i:i+2] == 'at':\n            at_positions.append(i)\n            i += 2\n        else:\n            i += 1\n    \n    # Generate all combinations of replacements\n    results = set()\n    \n    # For each combination of dot and at replacements\n    for dot_mask in product([0, 1], repeat=len(dot_positions)):\n        for at_mask in product([0, 1], repeat=len(at_positions)):\n            # Create a copy of the string\n            result = list(s)\n            \n            # Apply dot replacements (from right to left to preserve indices)\n            for i, pos in enumerate(reversed(dot_positions)):\n                if dot_mask[len(dot_positions) - 1 - i]:\n                    # Replace 'dot' with '.'\n                    result[pos] = '.'\n                    result[pos+1] = ''\n                    result[pos+2] = ''\n            \n            # Apply at replacements (from right to left to preserve indices)\n            for i, pos in enumerate(reversed(at_positions)):\n                if at_mask[len(at_positions) - 1 - i]:\n                    # Replace 'at' with '@'\n                    result[pos] = '@'\n                    result[pos+1] = ''\n            \n            # Clean up empty strings\n            final = ''.join([c for c in result if c != ''])\n            \n            if is_valid_email(final):\n                results.add(final)\n    \n    if not results:\n        # If no valid email found, return original (though problem implies there's always a solution)\n        return a\n    \n    # Find minimum length, then lexicographically smallest\n    min_len = min(len(r) for r in results)\n    candidates = [r for r in results if len(r) == min_len]\n    return min(candidates)\n\nprint generate_replacements(a)\n</repaired_code>\n<confidence>\n0.7\n</confidence>", "token_logprobs": [-4.768370445162873e-07, -0.0017769037513062358, -9.536738616588991e-07, -1.0728830375228426e-06, -0.25501665472984314, 0.0, -3.2186455882765586e-06, -8.344646857949556e-07, -0.009870058856904507, -0.252358525